{
  "reasoning": "I grouped the opening with the first substantive point, locating requirement gathering in the interactive system development life cycle, so the first segment is not only meta-lecturing. I then cut at clear bridges where a new sub-goal starts: sentence 15 for system-centered versus user-centered design, 47 for the calendar example of what counts as a usability requirement, 78 for non-functional requirements, 101 for knowing the user and the usability definition, 109 for contextual inquiry, 133 for an importance example, and 150 for the closing summary. The contextual inquiry importance segment is kept separate because it names contextual inquiry and restates that it involves observing user activities, so it can stand alone.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 1,
      "subgoal": "Describe the role of requirement gathering in the interactive system development life cycle as the stage where end-user usability requirements are identified."
    },
    {
      "start": 15,
      "subgoal": "Distinguish system-centered design from user-centered design by whose needs drive the design."
    },
    {
      "start": 47,
      "subgoal": "Identify end-user usability requirements as distinct from feasibility, interface design, and code design decisions using a calendar app example."
    },
    {
      "start": 78,
      "subgoal": "Explain usability requirements as non-functional interface-quality requirements relevant to interactive systems used by non-experts."
    },
    {
      "start": 101,
      "subgoal": "Explain why requirement gathering must begin with specified users and a specified context of use, using the ISO usability definition."
    },
    {
      "start": 109,
      "subgoal": "Describe the contextual inquiry method for gathering usability requirements by observing and interviewing users in their natural work context."
    },
    {
      "start": 133,
      "subgoal": "Explain why contextual inquiry is important for system development by showing how observed user activities determine essential features."
    },
    {
      "start": 150,
      "subgoal": "Summarize the main points of the lecture."
    }
  ]
}
